Z||000000||fotofrank||08-05-2000||04:48 PM||||So I was driving along and the car quit. Turn the key and the gauges do not work and the car does not start. No crank etc. Electric windows don't go up.What could it be?<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://fotofrank.homestead.com/foto1.html||||reg||8
Z||000001||rogergarrison||08-05-2000||05:02 PM||||do any lites work. If not sounds to me like battery or battery connections puked||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||08-05-2000||07:09 PM||||If everything is completely dead, check battery terminals (easiest), then junction block (on RH fender, aft of battery box), then fusible links.<P>(Above items represent an attempt to list them in increasing order of difficulty).<P>As always, Good Luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!||||reg||3
Z||000003||theogre||08-05-2000||08:15 PM||||check all the ground ties too. both the bat and the straps from block to body.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fotofrank||08-05-2000||11:33 PM||||the only thing that works are the curticy lights. Windows,radio etc do not work. Fusable links seem to check OK. An '84 Fiero is not wired like the rest of the years, ho terminal block. I've got power to the alternator but didn't check the starter.Had the car towed.(why cann't they brake in the garage at home?)||||reg||9
Z||000005||Standard||08-06-2000||06:19 AM||||electrical connections and fusible links going to the starter.. <P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 148,000 miles and counting!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||08-06-2000||09:37 AM||||happened to me once on my '87, but it would be pretty obvious if this happened. the way my pos. battery cable was routed had it close to the serpentine belt. it rubbed through and shorted out every thing. for what ever reason it started again after about ten minutes and i drove it home, found the problem and fixed it. had a similar poblem on my chevy that was just a loose battery connection. hope yours is just as easy.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fotofrank||08-07-2000||01:18 PM||||Well, all the easy fixes don't do it. It seems the fusable links are OK, at least the ones I tested. There is one from the regulator on the altinator to the battery that I may not have found or tested. The '84 Fieros are a different breed that all other Fieros. The shop has it now so I'll get to see $$$ fly. <P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://fotofrank.homestead.com/foto1.html||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroJon||08-07-2000||07:10 PM||||I had that same problem with my old '84 a few year back.  Ended up being a fusable link (one I didn't check).  Sorry I don't know which one it was, but cost about $100 to have fixed.<P>------------------<BR>Silver '86 2M4||||reg||3
Z||000009||Standard||08-08-2000||01:54 AM||||Yep, had the same problem on my 84 too.  It was a burned up fusible link at the starter.  replaced it and all is well..||||reg||1
Z||000010||fotofrank||08-08-2000||01:29 PM||||Well I went to get my car last nite. Tim (my machanic) at Larry and Dan's Marithon in Schaumburg IL, found a broken ignition wire. He test drove it ok but it failed for me. To day Tim found a cradle/engine mount bolt that had backed out and depending on the engine mevement would short out the 12Volts to the ignition. So far so good. I'll let you know how it turns out tonite. Nothing is ever easy, or simple on an '84 Fiero.:0<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://fotofrank.homestead.com/foto1.html||||reg||10
